The '''1982 Tava Morotí earthquake''' ({{lang-bl|Terremoto de Tava Morotí de 1982}}) occurred on 8 November at {{Tooltip|3:21p.m.|12:04 a.m. November 8 UTC}} local time. The shock was centered in the [[San Gabriel Nature Reserve]] in Castejón's [[Trinacria|Trinacria borough]], approximately {{convert|10|mi|km|0|abbr=on}} southeast of [[Santa Catalina, Sofia|Santa Catalina]] on a section of the [[Yawar Sypa]] fault system and was named for the nearby Tava Morotí valley.  With a [[W:Moment magnitude scale|M<sub>w</sub>]] magnitude of 7.1 and a maximum {{W|Modified Mercalli intensity scale|Modified Mercalli intensity}} of IX (''Violent''), the shock was responsible for 126 deaths and 8,722 injuries. This section of the Yawar Sypa Fault System had been relatively inactive since the [[1924 Alpujarra earthquake]] (to the degree that it was designated a {{W|seismic gap}}) until two moderate [[W:Foreshock|foreshocks]] in June and August 1982.

Damage was relatively heavy in Trinacria, which at the time was undergoing large-scale development following its [[1975 Trinacria referendum|annexation to Castejón]]. It was less damaging across [[Salto Bay]], but the earthquake's effects extended well across and outside of the metropolis. No surface faulting occurred, though {{W|soil liquefaction}} was a significant issue, especially in [[Sástago]], which was built atop former {{W|landfill}}.

The earthquake halted development in Trinacria, and greatly contributed to the dissolution of the [[Castejón Housing Authority]] in 1985. The earthquake profoundly changed Castejón's approach to automobiles (and transportation as a whole) as several elevated highway sections collapsed, resulting in a majority of the estimated 4,000 casualties. This ultimately led to all elevated sections of the metropolitan ''[[Autopista of Balisca|autopista]]'' being [[Low Impact Initiative|progressively demolished]], and the remainder [[Gran Excavación|moved underground]]. Damage to the region's transportation system was estimated at $1.8 billion (equivalent to $5.8 billion today). 

